Go interfaces are satisfied implicitly. The type declares its methods; the interface declares its method list; if the type's method set contains all of the interface's methods, the type satisfies it — and the compiler verifies this at the assignment where you use the type as the interface.

```go
type Reader interface{ Read() string }

type File struct{ name string }
func (f File) Read() string { return "reading " + f.name }

var r Reader = File{"a.txt"}
fmt.Println(r.Read())
```

Executed. No declaration connects File to Reader — not at File's definition, not at Reader's, nowhere. The fit is verified where it's used, and only there.

What this buys: interfaces can be defined AFTER the types that satisfy them. You can write an interface for types in a package you don't own — sort.Interface over your type, io.Reader over anything with a Read. The dependency arrow points the useful direction: consumers define the interface they need; producers just have methods.

The standard library is built on this: io.Reader (one method) is satisfied by files, network connections, buffers, cipher streams, HTTP bodies, decompressors — most written without any reference to io.Reader. The interface came later, and the fit was discovered, not declared.

Interface embedding builds bigger interfaces from smaller ones by unioning method lists:

```go
type Reader interface{ Read() string }
type Closer interface{ Close() error }

type ReadCloser interface {
    Reader    // embeds: adds Read
    Closer    // embeds: adds Close
}
```

Executed: File with Read and Close satisfies all three — Reader alone, Closer alone, and ReadCloser — because its method set covers each union.

The io package is the canonical showcase: io.ReadWriter, io.ReadCloser, io.ReadWriteCloser, io.ReadWriteSeeker — the standard interfaces are compositions of one- and two-method atoms. The atoms stay tiny; the compositions name the combinations programs actually need; and a type satisfying the atoms satisfies every composition automatically.

What embedding is NOT: inheritance. ReadCloser doesn't extend Reader — it lists Reader's methods alongside Closer's. No hierarchy, no overrides, no dispatch through the embedding; the composed method set is flat.

The design guidance: keep interfaces atom-sized, compose at use sites. Embed interfaces when a COMBINATION is genuinely a named thing (ReadCloser — a thing you read and close), not as a convenience for listing.
